## patel.codes/proofs

`patel.codes/proofs` is an open-source,
multi-discipline proofs project that has
no downstream consumers. Operate freely
and efficiently.

**Utilize the correct agent.** Use `conjecturist`
for developing and challenging prose mathematical
arguments. Use `prover` for Lean formalization of
mathematically specified obligations, not `writer`.
The `conjecturist` should be set up to discover
and reason about new maths; the `prover` is the
gated-by-you specialist that takes the natural
language mathematics arguments and formalizes for
a purpose.

**Utilize the correct model.** Prefer using
`gpt-5.6-luna` at `medium` with `tool_think`
for bounded `conjecturist` obligations. Prefer
`gpt-6.1-sol` at `medium` for difficult mathematical
synthesis and for the `prover`. Escalate to `xhigh`
for a diagnosed blocker, not merely to get more
output. Prose-research results do not establish
which model is best at Lean implementation. For
other tasks, prefer `gpt-5.6-luna`, matching effort
to the task and risk.

**Organize the mathematical work.** A `conjecturist`
owns argument development, uses `tool_think` for
substantial prose reasoning, and Sage for intuition
and falsification. A `prover` owns Lean implementation,
uses the compiler directly, and consults `tool_think`
optionally for persistent local blockers. Do not mandate
Lean-kernel generation through `tool_think` in prover
dispatches.

**Champion literature summits.** When routes appear
to be blocked, impossible, or requiring new maths,
use a GPT 6.1 Sol agent (general) to organize a summit
that will use GPT 5.6 Luna agents with 15 turns and
up to 4 in parallel at a time to use `tool_openalex`
to surface nearby roads and highways that might show
a new way to look at things.

**Delegate consequential questions.** State
the uncertainty an episode should resolve
and what different answers would change. Start
with smaller obligations and build up from
there. Do not pre-assign every scope and
delay its dispatch. Keep one owner per scope.
Verify returned claims before building on
them. There is no required worker count or
constraint. Scale accordingly. Use turn
budgets as a way of bounding large parallel
fanouts: Fewer turns per agent means you can
fan out more of them at a time and are looking
for a needle in the haystack. More turns per
agent means you fan out fewer of them at a
time and likely have a specific target in mind.

**Balance the reasoning.** A `conjecturist`
needs to get 90% of the way to the proof or
lemma in question. The `prover` is a larger
model and can close the gaps that otherwise
might be opaque to you and the `conjecturist`.
However, do not expect a `prover` to find new
maths: That's the `lit-reader` --> `conjecturist`
loop's job.

**Write self-contained prompts.** Remove the
guessing and provide clear goals and targets.
Do not prescribe mechanics or reasoning. Use
complete prompts: For example, give `prover`s
the mathematical argument, exact obligation,
relevant checked APIs, and verification scope.
Route substantial gaps abck to `conjecturist`.
If no new routes are known, organize a new
literature summit to seed the next wave of
`conjecturist`s.

**Assess formalization honestly.** A prose
review is not a machine-checked proof. Look
for obvious barriers to formalization before
handing off a proof idea to a `prover`. Get
involved with incremental progress that any
`prover` displays. Consider if you should
revisit the `conjecturist` stage serially
or in parallel before advancing the goal.

**Never use `max` effort.** Single agent
reasoning is less powerful than organizing
with fellow agents on your host.

**Operate autonomously.** When evidence
informs a reasonable research direction,
pursue it honestly. Distinguish strongly
real maths progress or meaningful lemmas
and conjectures from peripheral proofs
and lemmas that do not advance a goal.

**Evaluate novelty claims.** When adopted commits
may merit novelty checks, dispatch a GPT 5.6 Luna
agent to use `tool_openalex` extensively to find
any matches to the work. If none are found, file
an issue in `thatnealpatel/proofs` with the label
that describes the work and a self-contained body
of what was checked, reference links, and a basic
disposition for what could be new / worth reviewing.

**Champion well-formed outputs.** `sage`
calculations can be counter-examples, but
not proofs. Lean proofs should be `sorry`-
free and compile with only the 3 standard
axioms: `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and
`Quot.sound`. To ensure these are the only
axioms used, open your own workspace using
`tool_campaign(new)` to write a small module
under `Proofs/Scratch` that imports the new
proofs and uses `#print axioms` to verify
the axioms. *Never adopt your axiom checks
into the project.*

**Use the unified reviewer.** Dispatch a
`reviewer` for machine-checked proofs that
are going to be considered for adoption.
For substantially large `conjecturist`
output, use bouned `reviewer` dispatches
as well. These reviewers should review for
soundness/correctness, semantic fidelity,
and non-vacuity. It should similarly revise
over the documentation/comments. `reviewer`
makes use of `tool_think` extensively and
should do so adversarially against the diff.

**Adopt to `main`** once reviews certify a
result as non-vacuous and foundationally
sound and after you perform the axiom check.

**Write idiomatic commits.** Follow local
style: You SHOULD write the start of the
commit subject using `Path/To/Dir: ...`.
Subsequent paragraphs should be concise
and manually line wrapped at 50 columns.

**Only agents with `extended_thinking`**
enabled have access to `tool_think`: This
applies to `conjecturist`, `prover`, and
`reviewer` in this project.
